(1506997, 199217, 39850, '<td valign="top">7 - Wow.  No idea what this is, but the red is very good.  For me it is a little out of focus/blurred, and not enough detail.  I am sure if ''it'' were on something else also so richly colored, it would make a brilliant photo.</td>', '7 - Wow.  No idea what this is, but the red is very good.  For me it is a little out of focus/blurred, and not enough detail.  I am sure if ''it'' were on something else also so richly colored, it would make a brilliant photo.', '2005-07-05 01:55:15.000000', NULL, 1),
(1506999, 198284, 39850, '<td valign="top">6 - Nice warmth and detail/shine.  For me, would be better with a different crop.</td>', '6 - Nice warmth and detail/shine.  For me, would be better with a different crop.', '2005-07-05 01:55:52.000000', NULL, 1),
(1507000, 198719, 19982, '<td valign="top">The images that always suffer from a lack of comments are the ones that are right in the middle ground.  Everyone comments on the awesome shots.  Some comment on the bad shots. But the middle ones, very few people say anything.  So here I am to explain why I voted a 5:<br/> <br/>I actually think this is a decent shot.  It''s nice and sharp (fyi:  blurriness in the subject itself is the #1 reason that I''ve dropped an image below a 5).  It''s not a macro shot.  But it is "fairly close".  I think that closer still would have helped.  Get right down to one leaf.  Isolate the subject and make it important.  In this case, there are 3 somethings to look at so it sort of splits the attention.  The color is kinda cool...  I''ve never seen a leaf that color before and so, as images go, it''s fairly interesting.  But as for the "macro challenge" I could only call it average.  Get in a little closer next time and I''m sure you''ll score higher.  Hope this helps.<br/> <br/></td>', 'The images that always suffer from a lack of comments are the ones that are right in the middle ground.  Everyone comments on the awesome shots.  Some comment on the bad shots. But the middle ones, very few people say anything.  So here I am to explain why I voted a 5: I actually think this is a decent shot.  It''s nice and sharp (fyi:  blurriness in the subject itself is the #1 reason that I''ve dropped an image below a 5).  It''s not a macro shot.  But it is "fairly close".  I think that closer still would have helped.  Get right down to one leaf.  Isolate the subject and make it important.  In this case, there are 3 somethings to look at so it sort of splits the attention.  The color is kinda cool...  I''ve never seen a leaf that color before and so, as images go, it''s fairly interesting.  But as for the "macro challenge" I could only call it average.  Get in a little closer next time and I''m sure you''ll score higher.  Hope this helps. ', '2005-07-05 01:55:53.000000', NULL, 1),
(1507004, 199310, 39850, '<td valign="top">6 - Very nice colors.  For me, much better with one flower key focused and keeping that good depth you have there already.  Maybe just a different cropping, not sure.</td>', '6 - Very nice colors.  For me, much better with one flower key focused and keeping that good depth you have there already.  Maybe just a different cropping, not sure.', '2005-07-05 01:57:54.000000', NULL, 1),
(1507012, 199272, 19982, '<td valign="top">The images that always suffer from a lack of comments are the ones that are right in the middle ground.  Everyone comments on the awesome shots.  Some comment on the bad shots. But the middle ones, very few people say anything.  So here I am to explain why I voted a 5:<br/> <br/>The most important subject in this image is named in the title itself... the fly.  However, the fly is also the least sharp of the picture.  Two possibilities here:  1) You were just a tad too close and your lens couldn''t quite focus on the fly, or 2) the camera caught the leaf and focused on it instead of the fly.  For macros, sharpness in the subject is imperative, so really this is the only thing that, in my opinion, is keeping this image from being a better shot.<br/> <br/>Things I do like:  the fly is facing the camera.  Kudos!  I don''t know how many images I''ve seen where you''re looking straight down at the bug, or looking at it''s rear-end.  It ends up looking like its getting away.  Yours has a nice angle to it as well.  Again, kudos.  <br/> <br/>One other thing that could improve your score would be to get in even closer...  but you may have to save that idea for some new equipment.  Anyway, hopefully you had fun and I hope the comments help.</td>', 'The images that always suffer from a lack of comments are the ones that are right in the middle ground.  Everyone comments on the awesome shots.  Some comment on the bad shots. But the middle ones, very few people say anything.  So here I am to explain why I voted a 5: The most important subject in this image is named in the title itself... the fly.  However, the fly is also the least sharp of the picture.  Two possibilities here:  1) You were just a tad too close and your lens couldn''t quite focus on the fly, or 2) the camera caught the leaf and focused on it instead of the fly.  For macros, sharpness in the subject is imperative, so really this is the only thing that, in my opinion, is keeping this image from being a better shot. Things I do like:  the fly is facing the camera.  Kudos!  I don''t know how many images I''ve seen where you''re looking straight down at the bug, or looking at it''s rear-end.  It ends up looking like its getting away.  Yours has a nice angle to it as well.  Again, kudos.   One other thing that could improve your score would be to get in even closer...  but you may have to save that idea for some new equipment.  Anyway, hopefully you had fun and I hope the comments help.', '2005-07-05 02:02:07.000000', NULL, 1),
(1507014, 199354, 39850, '<td valign="top">9 - Yes.  No, you don''t really need to title a photo like this.  For me, it is just about perfect.  Perhaps the right antenna in focus, the eye more in focus, maybe a slightly different angle on the echinacea, the bee more centered, I don''t know.  Looks very close to perfect to me.</td>', '9 - Yes.  No, you don''t really need to title a photo like this.  For me, it is just about perfect.  Perhaps the right antenna in focus, the eye more in focus, maybe a slightly different angle on the echinacea, the bee more centered, I don''t know.  Looks very close to perfect to me.', '2005-07-05 02:04:17.000000', NULL, 1),
(1507015, 199306, 19982, '<td valign="top">The images that always suffer from a lack of comments are the ones that are right in the middle ground.  Everyone comments on the awesome shots.  Some comment on the bad shots. But the middle ones, very few people say anything.  So here I am to explain why I voted a 5:<br/> <br/>I think what keeps me from scoring this image higher is a lack of sharpness in at least one of the strawberries.  You''re "almost there".  The strawberry on the left is more focused than the one on the right (they don''t have to both be in focus, so you''re fine there).   You might also experiment a little with various angles.  This seems fine... but I''m wondering if a different angle might add a little more "pizazz" to the image.<br/> <br/>Anyway, the image isn''t really bad... it''s not as good as it could be either.  So that''s why I ended up giving a 5.  I hope the comments help.</td>', 'The images that always suffer from a lack of comments are the ones that are right in the middle ground.  Everyone comments on the awesome shots.  Some comment on the bad shots. But the middle ones, very few people say anything.  So here I am to explain why I voted a 5: I think what keeps me from scoring this image higher is a lack of sharpness in at least one of the strawberries.  You''re "almost there".  The strawberry on the left is more focused than the one on the right (they don''t have to both be in focus, so you''re fine there).   You might also experiment a little with various angles.  This seems fine... but I''m wondering if a different angle might add a little more "pizazz" to the image. Anyway, the image isn''t really bad... it''s not as good as it could be either.  So that''s why I ended up giving a 5.  I hope the comments help.', '2005-07-05 02:05:06.000000', NULL, 1),
(1507017, 198332, 39850, '<td valign="top">6 - Nice concept.  Given the title, for me it would be better with a front focal point.  A slightly different angle too perhaps, not sure.</td>', '6 - Nice concept.  Given the title, for me it would be better with a front focal point.  A slightly different angle too perhaps, not sure.', '2005-07-05 02:05:57.000000', NULL, 1),
(1507022, 198739, 39850, '<td valign="top">6 - I like this, however, focus on the fore of the tire, removing that little bit of background top left, a slight recentering, would make it better for me.</td>', '6 - I like this, however, focus on the fore of the tire, removing that little bit of background top left, a slight recentering, would make it better for me.', '2005-07-05 02:07:49.000000', NULL, 1),
(1507026, 196968, 39850, '<td valign="top">6 - Good concept but, for me, better if closer and more centered.</td>', '6 - Good concept but, for me, better if closer and more centered.', '2005-07-05 02:09:33.000000', NULL, 1),
(1507029, 199672, 19982, '<td valign="top">The images that always suffer from a lack of comments are the ones that are right in the middle ground.  Everyone comments on the awesome shots.  Some comment on the bad shots. But the middle ones, very few people say anything.  So here I am to explain why I voted a 5:<br/> <br/>I know... the challenge said close-ups as well as macros.  And maybe this critter truly is tiny.  But he is so far away that he doesn''t come close to filling the frame and that''s what is making it non-interesting in the middle of a macro challenge.  <br/> <br/>My suggestion, if your camera won''t let you get any closer, would be to crop the photo tighter.  Remove the top and bottom 3rds and the left and right 4ths and you''ll be about right.  However, in doing so, you''ll need to watch the resolution of the image because sharpness in the subject will become paramount.<br/> <br/>One more thing you could try, even without getting closer or making the grasshopper any larger...  put him in the bottom right quadrant of the image.  Doing so would give him space (to the left and to the top) to jump into.  He looks ready to jump, so give him room to go.  Dead center is the least interesting place to put him.  (and putting him on the far left would just be wrong)  :-)<br/> <br/>I hope you''ve had fun with the challenge and hopefully the comments help.</td>', 'The images that always suffer from a lack of comments are the ones that are right in the middle ground.  Everyone comments on the awesome shots.  Some comment on the bad shots. But the middle ones, very few people say anything.  So here I am to explain why I voted a 5: I know... the challenge said close-ups as well as macros.  And maybe this critter truly is tiny.  But he is so far away that he doesn''t come close to filling the frame and that''s what is making it non-interesting in the middle of a macro challenge.   My suggestion, if your camera won''t let you get any closer, would be to crop the photo tighter.  Remove the top and bottom 3rds and the left and right 4ths and you''ll be about right.  However, in doing so, you''ll need to watch the resolution of the image because sharpness in the subject will become paramount. One more thing you could try, even without getting closer or making the grasshopper any larger...  put him in the bottom right quadrant of the image.  Doing so would give him space (to the left and to the top) to jump into.  He looks ready to jump, so give him room to go.  Dead center is the least interesting place to put him.  (and putting him on the far left would just be wrong)  :-) I hope you''ve had fun with the challenge and hopefully the comments help.', '2005-07-05 02:11:29.000000', NULL, 1),
(1507041, 197963, 39850, '<td valign="top">9 - Ohh, wow.  Wonderful colors.  Great shot of the flower.  Good shot of the bee.  For me the flowers actually dominate this picture, which draws my attention away from the lack of fine detail in the bee.  Very nice photo.  More detail on the bee maybe, but then you might lose the flower.  Love the detail there in the centre of the ''flower''.  You have made clover look very pretty and captured its character extremely well.  Now I am wondering about ''pp'' (re colors).  I will come back to see how you did this.</td>', '9 - Ohh, wow.  Wonderful colors.  Great shot of the flower.  Good shot of the bee.  For me the flowers actually dominate this picture, which draws my attention away from the lack of fine detail in the bee.  Very nice photo.  More detail on the bee maybe, but then you might lose the flower.  Love the detail there in the centre of the ''flower''.  You have made clover look very pretty and captured its character extremely well.  Now I am wondering about ''pp'' (re colors).  I will come back to see how you did this.', '2005-07-05 02:17:37.000000', NULL, 1),
