(1507641, 199497, 36499, '<td valign="top">excellent macro here and great colors. only thing i dont like is that  his wing tip is cut off. you captured awesone detail here...9</td>', 'excellent macro here and great colors. only thing i dont like is that  his wing tip is cut off. you captured awesone detail here...9', '2005-07-05 10:54:09.000000', NULL, 1),
(1507648, 197970, 19982, '<td valign="top">The images that always suffer from a lack of comments are the ones that are right in the middle ground.  Everyone comments on the awesome shots.  Some comment on the bad shots. But the middle ones, very few people say anything.  So here I am to explain why I voted a 5:<br/> <br/>Looking at the wing and the lower legs you can see that perfect focus on part of the dragonfly was achieved.  This is very hard to do and your accomplishment in this regard should be acknowledged!<br/> <br/>For me, the critical part of a macro *is* the sharpness in the details.  But in this case, I think you missed my only a fraction of an inch.  The eyes and the head do not appear to be in focus.  And yet the branch in front of the head looks fine.  This makes me think the problem wasn''t so much the focus (well, it *does* have to do with "where" you focused), but that a smaller aperture, resulting in greater depth of field, would have helped bring all of the essential parts into sharp focus.<br/> <br/>I also happen to like the composition on this one.  The angled view of the dragonfly as well as the matching angle of its background which opposes the angle of the stick it is holding onto.<br/> <br/>So while this is not a bad shot, the lack of focus on the head and eye is what hurts the shot, and that''s why I ended up giving it a 5.  Hope this helps.</td>', 'The images that always suffer from a lack of comments are the ones that are right in the middle ground.  Everyone comments on the awesome shots.  Some comment on the bad shots. But the middle ones, very few people say anything.  So here I am to explain why I voted a 5: Looking at the wing and the lower legs you can see that perfect focus on part of the dragonfly was achieved.  This is very hard to do and your accomplishment in this regard should be acknowledged! For me, the critical part of a macro *is* the sharpness in the details.  But in this case, I think you missed my only a fraction of an inch.  The eyes and the head do not appear to be in focus.  And yet the branch in front of the head looks fine.  This makes me think the problem wasn''t so much the focus (well, it *does* have to do with "where" you focused), but that a smaller aperture, resulting in greater depth of field, would have helped bring all of the essential parts into sharp focus. I also happen to like the composition on this one.  The angled view of the dragonfly as well as the matching angle of its background which opposes the angle of the stick it is holding onto. So while this is not a bad shot, the lack of focus on the head and eye is what hurts the shot, and that''s why I ended up giving it a 5.  Hope this helps.', '2005-07-05 10:57:59.000000', NULL, 1),
(1507650, 198700, 19982, '<td valign="top">Certainly not a "macro"...  but this is a decent close-up and all the necessary parts are in sharp focus.  Originally scored a 5, but bumping up to 6.</td>', 'Certainly not a "macro"...  but this is a decent close-up and all the necessary parts are in sharp focus.  Originally scored a 5, but bumping up to 6.', '2005-07-05 10:59:01.000000', NULL, 1),
(1507653, 198931, 24314, '<td valign="top">beautifully crisp, clear and just stunning! you should make this available as a print as I''m sure it would do very well! 10</td>', 'beautifully crisp, clear and just stunning! you should make this available as a print as I''m sure it would do very well! 10', '2005-07-05 11:01:37.000000', NULL, 1),
(1507654, 199656, 19982, '<td valign="top">The images that always suffer from a lack of comments are the ones that are right in the middle ground.  Everyone comments on the awesome shots.  Some comment on the bad shots. But the middle ones, very few people say anything.  So here I am to explain why I voted a 5:<br/> <br/>The essential thing in a macro is sharpness in the details.  And while this image comes very close, I feel that it fails to be as sharp as it could be around the mosquitto''s head and abdomen.<br/> <br/>Another thing that could help in the area of composition would be to move just a bit more to the front.  Continue to allow a front-to-back side view, but make the "point of impact" (where the "daytime vampire" is doing his dirty work) the focal point of the picture.<br/> <br/>Hope the comments help.</td>', 'The images that always suffer from a lack of comments are the ones that are right in the middle ground.  Everyone comments on the awesome shots.  Some comment on the bad shots. But the middle ones, very few people say anything.  So here I am to explain why I voted a 5: The essential thing in a macro is sharpness in the details.  And while this image comes very close, I feel that it fails to be as sharp as it could be around the mosquitto''s head and abdomen. Another thing that could help in the area of composition would be to move just a bit more to the front.  Continue to allow a front-to-back side view, but make the "point of impact" (where the "daytime vampire" is doing his dirty work) the focal point of the picture. Hope the comments help.', '2005-07-05 11:02:00.000000', NULL, 1),
