(1526221, 199291, 28710, '<td valign="top">Awesome shot. I wasnt'' able to vote on it, but if I had, this would have been my pick for the blue. I have always loved the coloring of fresh blueberries. The surface you placed them on is excellent as well. The lighting is soft and beautiful. I wonder how much post processing you did. Truly outstanding.<br/> <br/>I love the massiveness of the berries. It reminds me of back home... <br/> <br/>A big smack in the face to the one guy who rated this a 3.</td>', 'Awesome shot. I wasnt'' able to vote on it, but if I had, this would have been my pick for the blue. I have always loved the coloring of fresh blueberries. The surface you placed them on is excellent as well. The lighting is soft and beautiful. I wonder how much post processing you did. Truly outstanding. I love the massiveness of the berries. It reminds me of back home...  A big smack in the face to the one guy who rated this a 3.', '2005-07-11 10:57:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(1526222, 198481, 32792, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by carodani:</b></div><hr/><i> the right spelling is bolognese </i></td></tr></table> <br/>Just to add, that might be the correct spelling in ENGLISH, but in portuguese, the title is absolutely correct!</td>', 'Originally posted by carodani: the right spelling is bolognese  Just to add, that might be the correct spelling in ENGLISH, but in portuguese, the title is absolutely correct!', '2005-07-11 10:58:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(1526226, 199170, 28710, '<td valign="top">I''d love to know what it is that you bribed that fly with to make him flap his wings and pose so nicely for you to let that flash light his face so pleasantly.<br/> <br/>Mind Boggling. I agree that this really should have blown away the competition. I also feel that the top three were pretty average shots. I would have said Blueberries, this then the Warrior wasp.<br/> <br/></td>', 'I''d love to know what it is that you bribed that fly with to make him flap his wings and pose so nicely for you to let that flash light his face so pleasantly. Mind Boggling. I agree that this really should have blown away the competition. I also feel that the top three were pretty average shots. I would have said Blueberries, this then the Warrior wasp. ', '2005-07-11 11:02:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(1526236, 198803, 5354, '<td valign="top">WOW, what can I say, thanks to you all including yurasocolov. I do appreciate all your comments. The image is not very imaginative, I new there would be lots of bugs, lots of water droplets and lots of flowers and to get points over any of these the winner would have to be brilliant at any one of these. I knew that by combining these themes that there were extra marks to be gained and I had seen something similar a long time back on some other site (can''t find it now though). I did really enjoy experimenting with this and building a small ''set'' so achieve the effects.<br/> <br/>So for those of you who asked about the details here is how:<br/> <br/>Equipment:<br/>1 small cardboard box - approx 9inches across.<br/>1 Sheet dark green card for backdrop, applied to inside of box<br/>1 spiral reed pushed through holes in side of box - used spiral reed in order to stop the water droplet running away<br/>1 white/pink/purple daisy like flower - pushed through a hole in back of box<br/>several willing and unwilling ladybirds<br/>1 childs paintbrush - to coax ladybird into walking the plank<br/>1 syringe to add water after each retry<br/>camera - 10D with 100mm f2.8 macro lens<br/>canon ex550 flash with remote trigger<br/>tripod<br/> <br/>OK so with the reed and the flower set up I added a drop of water, then pushed/pulled the flower until I got the right amount of blur in the background whilst manually focussing on the water drop.<br/> <br/>Once this was set the it was a case of ladybird wrangling with the pant brush. I found that they didn''t like to pass the water droplet and would either turn round or jump off at that point. I know I had about 7 or 8 bugs and only got 3 or 4 decent shots before I ran out.<br/> <br/>I also tried the same shot with a small froglet, but the dof was too short to get his nose and feet in focus, so I went back to the ladybirds. I also had an attempt at a damsel fly version, but he wasn''t to sure about signing the model release so I had to let him go.<br/> <br/>All done in a very enjoyable afternoon sat in the garden.</td>', 'WOW, what can I say, thanks to you all including yurasocolov. I do appreciate all your comments. The image is not very imaginative, I new there would be lots of bugs, lots of water droplets and lots of flowers and to get points over any of these the winner would have to be brilliant at any one of these. I knew that by combining these themes that there were extra marks to be gained and I had seen something similar a long time back on some other site (can''t find it now though). I did really enjoy experimenting with this and building a small ''set'' so achieve the effects. So for those of you who asked about the details here is how: Equipment:1 small cardboard box - approx 9inches across.1 Sheet dark green card for backdrop, applied to inside of box1 spiral reed pushed through holes in side of box - used spiral reed in order to stop the water droplet running away1 white/pink/purple daisy like flower - pushed through a hole in back of boxseveral willing and unwilling ladybirds1 childs paintbrush - to coax ladybird into walking the plank1 syringe to add water after each retrycamera - 10D with 100mm f2.8 macro lenscanon ex550 flash with remote triggertripod OK so with the reed and the flower set up I added a drop of water, then pushed/pulled the flower until I got the right amount of blur in the background whilst manually focussing on the water drop. Once this was set the it was a case of ladybird wrangling with the pant brush. I found that they didn''t like to pass the water droplet and would either turn round or jump off at that point. I know I had about 7 or 8 bugs and only got 3 or 4 decent shots before I ran out. I also tried the same shot with a small froglet, but the dof was too short to get his nose and feet in focus, so I went back to the ladybirds. I also had an attempt at a damsel fly version, but he wasn''t to sure about signing the model release so I had to let him go. All done in a very enjoyable afternoon sat in the garden.', '2005-07-11 11:04:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(1526238, 197333, 34980, '<td valign="top">congrats!!! keep up the good work.</td>', 'congrats!!! keep up the good work.', '2005-07-11 11:05:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(1526256, 198866, 28710, '<td valign="top">Absolutely stunning! And hilarious!<br/> <br/>I felt a bit funny about the background though, not sure what it was.</td>', 'Absolutely stunning! And hilarious! I felt a bit funny about the background though, not sure what it was.', '2005-07-11 11:11:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(1526265, 198815, 3087, '<td valign="top">B/W Mentor Group.<br/> <br/>This is an unusual shot and I think it lends itself well to black and white. Here are my observations, in no particular order:<br/>- The toning applied to the black and white seems rather red in colour, this may be deliberate to echo the colour of the wine.<br/>- Although there are some really bright white areas outside the window, because there are a lot of highlights in the foreground objects this doesn''t stand out or distract particularly.<br/>- I like the way the objects are placed to one side to allow the view of the window grills but I find their placement somewhat cluttered. <br/>- I wonder if more could be made of the reflections of the objects in the surface material?<br/>- It''s hard to focus on the shapes and outline of the flowers and leaves because of the strong shapes of the grill just behind.<br/>- I like the detail in the glass of the objects and the reflections in the wine glass are particularly intriguing.<br/>- I like the way the bottom left hand corner is punctuated by a full circle from the grill. I think the slight cropping make the one at the top left less effective.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2005-07-11 10:44:40.</i></td>', 'B/W Mentor Group. This is an unusual shot and I think it lends itself well to black and white. Here are my observations, in no particular order:- The toning applied to the black and white seems rather red in colour, this may be deliberate to echo the colour of the wine.- Although there are some really bright white areas outside the window, because there are a lot of highlights in the foreground objects this doesn''t stand out or distract particularly.- I like the way the objects are placed to one side to allow the view of the window grills but I find their placement somewhat cluttered. - I wonder if more could be made of the reflections of the objects in the surface material?- It''s hard to focus on the shapes and outline of the flowers and leaves because of the strong shapes of the grill just behind.- I like the detail in the glass of the objects and the reflections in the wine glass are particularly intriguing.- I like the way the bottom left hand corner is punctuated by a full circle from the grill. I think the slight cropping make the one at the top left less effective.', '2005-07-11 11:14:48.000000', '2005-07-11 11:44:40.000000', 0),
(1526273, 199048, 28710, '<td valign="top">This is to me one of the few actually really creative shots. I appreciate the shots that took advantage of great opportunities, but to make something so great from simple house hold goods, that is creativity.<br/> <br/>I have always liked the look of cinnamon sticks. It is indeed one of my favorite spices (and embarassingly one I know sadly little about). <br/> <br/>I think I''m going to go do some research on cinnamon now!<br/> <br/>Great job!</td>', 'This is to me one of the few actually really creative shots. I appreciate the shots that took advantage of great opportunities, but to make something so great from simple house hold goods, that is creativity. I have always liked the look of cinnamon sticks. It is indeed one of my favorite spices (and embarassingly one I know sadly little about).  I think I''m going to go do some research on cinnamon now! Great job!', '2005-07-11 11:16:47.000000', NULL, 0),
